💡 **SUGGESTION: unify machine-identity normalization across the two trust paths (trailing `[bot]`)**

The parse side strips one trailing `[bot]` on both sides of the allowlist match (`trustedLogin`, gh-1025), but the new thread-side helpers compare raw lowercased logins:

- `normalizedMachineLogins` (this line) — `String(l).toLowerCase()`, no `[bot]` normalization
- `threadSkipReason` — `String(t.author || '').toLowerCase()` vs `logins.indexOf(author)`

`machineSmAgent.resolveSuggestionThreads` feeds the **same** `cfg.machineLogins` list to both paths (`provider.verdictRecords({authorLogins: logins})` and `selectResolvableThreads({machineLogins: logins})`). With a bare App login configured (e.g. `dmtools-factory-bot`), a verdict marker authored by the App identity `dmtools-factory-bot[bot]` is trusted for the gate — but the same identity's threads then fail the authorship check, skip as `human`, and the machine never resolves its own non-blocking threads (the gh-828 stall, per-thread flavor).

This is pre-existing semantics faithfully preserved by the refactor (the extraction moved the old inline `selectResolvableThreads` logic verbatim, and the new test pins `AI-Teammate` case-insensitivity), so it does not block this PR. A follow-up reusing `trustedLogin` on both sides would leave one normalization definition for both trust paths:

```js
function normalizedMachineLogins(opts) {
    var logins = [];
    ((opts && opts.machineLogins) || []).forEach(function (l) {
        if (l) logins.push(trustedLogin(l));
    });
    return logins;
}
// threadSkipReason: trustedLogin(t.author) === trustedLogin(entry) comparison
```

`trustedLogin` is already in this module — no new dependency, and the strict "different bot never matches" property is kept.

